In a discussion about applying a PPP (Purchasing Power Parity) conversion to MER (Market Exchange Rate) data over multiple years, James asked whether to use different conversion rates for each year or a single rate, similar to real unit dollars. I advised that it is necessary to use a different conversion rate for each year, as PPP rates vary annually due to inflation and economic changes. Each year's rate accurately reflects the purchasing power and economic conditions of that specific year, making it crucial for proper long-term economic analysis. I recommended using time series data of PPP conversion factors to ensure accuracy in comparisons.
